<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vichy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vichy is a spa town on the Allier in the Auvergne, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, known since Roman times for its mineral springs. After the defeat of 1940 the French government settled there because of its many hotels, and parliament, meeting in its casino, voted full powers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a> on 10 July 1940, so that his collaborationist regime of 1940–1944 became known as Vichy France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vichy.jpg" alt="Vichy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Kowalski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2023-07-23_-_vue_sur_Vichy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vichy is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Allier, on the river Allier, with about 25,000 inhabitants. Its mineral springs are rich in trace elements such as lithium and fluorine and high in sodium bicarbonate. They owe their warmth to volcanic activity in the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a>, and about 289 springs have been charted in the town and its surroundings. The name is the French form of the Occitan Vichèi, of uncertain etymology; one proposal derives it from a Latin Vippiacus, after a landowner presumably called Vippius. The cold soup vichyssoise is named after the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known settlement was founded by Roman legionaries in 52 BCE, who found the hot springs on their way south after their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>. They called the township Aquae Calidae, &quot;Hot Waters&quot;. In 1344 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-france">John II of France</a> ceded the fiefdom to Peter I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, and by 1374 it was wholly in the House of Bourbon's hands. A Celestinian monastery was founded in 1410. By the end of the 16th century the baths drew patients from the noble and wealthy classes, and the marquise de Sévigné, a patient in 1676 and 1677, popularised them in her letters.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s stays between 1861 and 1866 transformed the town: dikes were built along the Allier, 13 hectares of gardens replaced the marshes, chalets and pavilions were built for his court, and a large casino opened in 1865. The Opera House and the Hall of Springs were inaugurated in 1903. Vichy welcomed 40,000 curistes in 1900 and nearly 100,000 just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and thermal life reached its height in the 1930s.</p><p>After the defeat of 1940 the French government fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and, finding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont-Ferrand</a> too cramped, moved to Vichy on 1 July. The town lay in the unoccupied zone and had the second largest hotel capacity in the country. On 9 and 10 July, 670 of the 846 members of the Senate and Chamber of Deputies voted full powers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a>. The new regime called itself the French State but became known as Vichy France. Germany occupied the Free Zone in November 1942. The city was liberated without bloodshed on 26 August 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 2021 Vichy became part of the transnational UNESCO World Heritage Site Great Spa Towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Vichy Pastilles are octagonal candies made from the soda in the spring waters, and the name Vichy is used for a brand of the L'Oréal company. A term of the war years must not be confused with its inhabitants: partisans of the Pétain regime are called Vichyste, while residents are Vichyssois.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vichy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Admiral Jean Decoux governed for <b>Vichy</b> France from 1940 to 1945, promoting youth and sports movements and local patriotism loyal to Marshal Pétain while sharing the colony with Japanese forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 July 1940 the French parliament gave full powers to Marshal Philippe Pétain, who proclaimed the French State at <b>Vichy</b>, while Charles de Gaulle called on the French to rally to Free France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In occupied Europe Germany ruled through annexation, Reich commissariats in Norway, the Netherlands and the Soviet territories, military administrations, and collaborating regimes such as <b>Vichy</b> France and the Independent State of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty of independence negotiated in 1936 under President Hashim al-Atassi was never ratified by France; British and Free French forces took the country from <b>Vichy</b> in July 1941, and French troops left in April 1946.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vichy</b> replaced Marianne with Joan of Arc as the national symbol of France, and its &quot;francisque&quot; insignia arranged a Gallic baton and double-headed hatchet to resemble the fasces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Pétain’s <b>Vichy</b> regime of 1940–1944 combined authoritarian conservatism with heightened antisemitism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
